    {"id": "arxiv-2205.15659",
     "title": "The CLRS Algorithmic Reasoning Benchmark",
     "authors": ["Petar Veličković", "Adrià Puigdomènech Badia", "David Budden", "Razvan Pascanu", "Andrea Banino",
                 "Misha Dashevskiy", "Raia Hadsell", "Charles Blundell"],
     "year": 2022, "version": "arXiv v2",
     "links": {"arxiv": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.15659", "code": "https://github.com/deepmind/clrs"},
     "tasks": ["algorithm-learning"],
     "fam": ["graph-neural-networks", "deep-learning"], "par": ["supervised"],
     "proposes": ["CLRS-30 benchmark: input/output and intermediate 'hint' trajectories for 30 algorithms from the CLRS textbook (sorting, searching, divide and conquer, greedy, dynamic programming, graphs, strings, geometry) in a graph encoding, with a fixed in-distribution validation (16 nodes) and out-of-distribution test (64 nodes) protocol; baseline processors Deep Sets, GAT, Memory Networks, MPNN, PGN"],
     "claims": [
         ("Contribution: CLRS-30 is a dataset of trajectories (inputs, outputs and optional intermediate targets) for 30 classical algorithms covering sorting, searching, dynamic programming, geometry, graphs and strings, proposed to unify evaluation that prior work did on bespoke data.",
          "Introduction",
          "we propose and evaluate on CLRS-30: a dataset containing trajectories—a trajectory is formed of inputs, the corresponding outputs and optional intermediary targets—of 30 classical algorithms"),
         ("Sorting algorithms included: insertion sort, bubble sort, heapsort and quicksort. (Searching includes minimum, binary search and quickselect; merge sort is excluded because it needs memory not attached to input objects.)",
          "CLRS Algorithmic Reasoning Benchmark",
          "Sorting: Insertion sort, bubble sort, heapsort (Williams, 1964), quicksort (Hoare, 1962)."),
         ("Greedy algorithms included: activity selection and task scheduling.",
          "CLRS Algorithmic Reasoning Benchmark",
          "Greedy: Activity selection (Gavril, 1972), task scheduling (Lawler, 1985)."),
         ("Train vs test sizes: training and validation inputs have 16 nodes (1,000 training and 32 validation trajectories; validation measures in-distribution generalisation), and the test set measures out-of-distribution generalisation with 32 trajectories on inputs of 64 nodes (more for graph-level outputs). Most graph-algorithm inputs are Erdos-Renyi graphs; scalar inputs are U(0,1).",
          "Dataset statistics",
          "For testing, we measure out-of-distribution generalisation, and sample 32 trajectories for inputs of 64 nodes."),
         ("Supervision with hints: baselines both decode hints (in the loss) and encode them as inputs, with noisy teacher forcing at training time (ground-truth hints fed back with probability 0.5); at evaluation the number of processor steps is set by the number of ground-truth hints, a requirement the authors say could be lifted, e.g., with termination networks.",
          "Baseline models",
          "The quantity of hints is still used to determine the number of processor steps to perform at evaluation time."),
         ("Out-of-distribution failure: MPNNs, which appear to dominate in-distribution for most tasks (Section 4.3: over 90% F1 for nearly all of them on validation), do not transfer their gains to graphs four times larger (64 vs 16 nodes); PGN becomes the most performant model when averaged across task types, which the authors say aligns with prior research (Veličković et al., 2020).",
          "Test (out-of-distribution) performance",
          "MPNNs are unable to transfer their impressive gains to graphs that are four times larger"),
         ("Stated failure modes: the authors say the OOD version of CLRS-30 is highly challenging and far from solved for most tasks; PGNs struggled on tasks requiring long-range rollouts (e.g. DFS) or recursive reasoning (e.g. Quicksort, Quickselect); string matching (e.g. KMP) may need more specialised inductive biases; the processor tended to do best on tasks with favourable (sublinear) hint counts such as BFS, Bellman-Ford and task scheduling.",
          "Test (out-of-distribution) performance",
          "In particular, PGNs struggled on tasks requiring long-range rollouts (such as DFS), or recursive reasoning (such as Quicksort and Quickselect)."),
     ],
     "notes": "Affiliation: all authors DeepMind (title page). PDF states: Proceedings of the 39th International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2022), PMLR 162. Full algorithm list (Section 3): Sorting (insertion, bubble, heapsort, quicksort); Searching (minimum, binary search, quickselect); D&C (maximum subarray, Kadane's variant: one algorithm); Greedy (activity selection, task scheduling); DP (matrix chain multiplication, LCS, optimal BST); Graphs (DFS, BFS, topological sort, articulation points, bridges, Kosaraju SCC, Kruskal, Prim, Bellman-Ford, Dijkstra, DAG shortest paths, Floyd-Warshall); Strings (naive matching, KMP); Geometry (segment intersection, Graham scan, Jarvis' march). Exclusions: NP-hard tasks and approximation algorithms, tasks needing numerical outputs, standalone data structures, algorithms needing dynamically allocated memory (merge sort, Hierholzer, finite-automaton string matching). Sorting is encoded as predicting predecessor pointers (no value swapping); insertion sort's inner backward scan is compressed into one step. Metric: test micro-F1 averaged per algorithm class (Table 1), plus a win/tie/loss count across 30 algorithms (Appendix C: model A outperforms B on an algorithm if mean(A) - std(A) > mean(B)); validation curves averaged over three seeds. Training: batch 32, Adam lr 0.001, 10,000 steps, early stopping on in-distribution validation, one V100. Hyperparameters: 'We provide all implementations and hyperparameters within our codebase'; no per-model tuning procedure is described in the text. Authors cite Xu et al. (arxiv-1905.13211) on algorithmic alignment and Veličković et al. 2019 (arxiv-1910.10593) for the MPNN max aggregator. The first author is also first author of the cited works that introduced baseline components used here (Veličković et al., 2019 for the max-aggregator MPNN executor; Veličković et al., 2020 for PGN; he is also first author of the GAT paper used as a baseline processor); Hadsell and Blundell are co-authors of arxiv-1910.10593 too (reference list). CLRS's statement that better-aligned architectures 'can be proven to have better sample complexity' (Xu et al.) should be read with the assumptions of that proof (arxiv-1905.13211 c3: sequential training with auxiliary labels)."},

